Critical: Always respect standard operator precedence (PEMDAS) when evaluating expressions. Multiplication and division bind tighter than addition and subtraction. When writing a custom evaluator (instead of using eval), implement two-level evaluation: first compute all * and / operations, then combine with + and -. Never assume left-to-right evaluation for mixed operators. Python's built-in eval handles this correctly, so prefer it for brute-force enumeration. (Reference: Q2, Q3, Q5)
</description>
<example>
`15 + 19 - 8 * 2 + 24`: Multiplication first gives 8*2=16, then left-to-right: 15+19=34, 34-16=18, 18+24=42. A naive left-to-right evaluator would wrongly compute 15+19=34, 34-8=26, 26*2=52, 52+24=76.
</example>
</memory_item>
