<description>
When writing a custom expression evaluator for operator-insertion problems, be careful to handle all operator combinations correctly and respect precedence. A common bug is an incomplete custom evaluator that fails on certain operator mixes (e.g., returning "No solution found" incorrectly). When in doubt, use Python's eval() which correctly handles precedence, and wrap in try/except for ZeroDivisionError and SyntaxError. (Reference: Q3)
</description>
<example>
The buggy custom evaluate() function in Q3 incorrectly returned "No solution found" for 19 ? 8 ? 28 = -205. Switching to eval(f"19 {op1} 8 {op2} 28") correctly found 19 - 8 * 28 = -205.
